# [Is Subsequence](https://leetcode.com/problems/is-subsequence/)

## Approach: Two Pointers

We traverse string `t` and check whether the characters of string `s` appear in the same order.

- Maintain `index` to track the next character to match in `s`.
- If `t[i] == s[index]`, increment `index`.
- If all characters of `s` are matched, return `true`.
- Otherwise, return `false` after traversing `t`.

### Code (C++)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isSubsequence(string s, string t) {
        int index = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < t.length(); i++) {
            if (index < s.length() && t[i] == s[index]) {
                index++;
            }

            if (index == s.length()) return true;
        }

        return index == s.length();
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\), where \(n\) is the length of `t`.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(1)\), as only a constant amount of extra space is used.

### Key Takeaway

A subsequence preserves the relative order of characters but does not require them to be contiguous.

**Edge case:** If `s` is empty, it is always a subsequence of `t`, so the function correctly returns `true`.
